body:
CSS Color 4 introduce the [color-interpolation-method](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-4/#color-interpolation-method) production and CSS Images 4 has adopted it for gradients (see for how it is incorporated into linear gradients, https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images-4/#linear-gradients, but it is also applied to radial and conic gradients) which allows gradients to have their color stops interpolated in a non-sRGB color space, leading to often more pleasing results (subjective of course). This would also be a good opportunity to bring support for premultiplied alpha interpolation (which is what CSS uses exclusively) to allow further unification.

Bringing this functionality to canvas would be quite useful for all the same reasons it is useful for CSS.

My straw man proposal would be to add properties to the existing [CanvasGradient](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/canvas.html#canvasgradient) object along the lines of:

```idl
enum ColorInterpolationMethod {
  "srgb",
  "srgb-linear",
  "lab",
  "oklab",
  "xyz",
  "xyz-d50",
  "xyz-d65",
  "hsl",
  "hwb",
  "lch",
  "oklch"
 };

enum HueInterpolationMethod {
  "shorter",
  "longer",
  "increasing",
  "decreasing",
  "specified"
}

enum AlphaPremultiplication {
  "premultiplied",
  "unpremultiplied" // (maybe "non-premultiplied" instead?
}

interface CanvasGradient {
  undefined addColorStop(double offset, DOMString color);

  attribute ColorInterpolationMethod colorInterpolationMethod; // (default "srgb")
  attribute HueInterpolationMethod hueInterpolationMethod; // (default "shorter", only applicable to "hsl", "hwb", "lch" and "oklch")
  attribute AlphaPremultiplication alpha; // (default "non-premultiplied")
};
```
